<file path="task_plan.md">
# Task Plan: "Apply to site" toggle — live-apply design tokens to the app UI

## 1. Goal

Add a toggle to the palette generator that applies the user's current design tokens
(palette colors + typography) directly to the website's own UI chrome, live and fully
reversible. Toggling off (or leaving the page) restores the site's default theme.

## 2. Context (verified against the codebase)

- App chrome theming: flat shadcn-style CSS custom properties defined in
  `app/globals.css` `:root` (lines 8–30) with dark overrides in a
  `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` block (lines 32–55). Tokens:
  `--background`, `--foreground`, `--surface`, `--surface-elevated`, `--surface-muted`,
  `--muted`, `--border`, `--input`, `--primary`, `--primary-foreground`,
  `--primary-hover`, `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--accent`,
  `--accent-foreground`, `--accent-subtle`, `--accent-subtle-foreground`,
  `--destructive`, `--destructive-foreground`, `--ring`, `--overlay`.
  A Tailwind v4 `@theme inline` block (lines 57–81) maps each token to utilities
  (`bg-primary`, `text-foreground`, …) and maps `--font-sans: var(--font-google-sans)`.
- Generator state lives in `components/generator/palette-generator.tsx`:
  `roles: ColorRole[]`, `typography: Typography`, derived `palettes: Palette[]`
  (11-stop scales, e.g. `--primary-600`) and `cssVars` applied inline to the
  workspace div. Previews consume scale vars via inline styles
  (`components/generator/previews/*`, `preview-overlay.tsx`).
- There is **no** runtime theme mechanism today (no next-themes, no
  `documentElement.style.setProperty`, no iframe). `app/providers.tsx` has only
  QueryClient + Auth providers.
- Key gap: the generator's 11-stop scale namespace and the app's flat token
  namespace are disjoint. A scale→flat semantic mapping must be written.
- Tailwind v4 cannot mint utility classes at runtime, but overriding the *values*
  of existing custom properties at runtime re-resolves every utility live. This is
  the intended mechanism.
- `components/generator/generator-toolbar.tsx` is dead code; the real toolbar is
  inline in `palette-generator.tsx` (~lines 504–555).
- Runtime Google Fonts loading already exists:
  `components/generator/font-preview-loader.tsx` — reuse for applied typography.
- Constraint from AGENTS.md: this Next.js (16.2.9, React 19.2.4) differs from
  training data — consult `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before writing code.
  Feature is entirely client-side, so no server API surface is touched.

## 3. Design decisions

### 3.1 Mechanism: `:root` CSS-variable override (the "sandbox" question)

Apply the mapped tokens by setting inline custom properties on
`document.documentElement` while the toggle is ON, and removing exactly those
properties when OFF / on unmount. This is effectively a sandbox in the sense that
matters: non-destructive, scoped to custom-property values, and instantly
reversible — no stylesheet edits, no persistence, no server involvement.

An `<iframe srcdoc>` sandbox was considered and rejected for the MVP: the point of
the feature is to see the tokens on the *real* site chrome, and an iframe would
require duplicating the app shell inside it (large, fragile, and new
infrastructure). If we later need hard isolation, the mapping function is reusable
inside an iframe unchanged.

### 3.2 Deterministic mapping, AI as optional follow-up

The scale→flat mapping is deterministic (pure function, unit-testable, instant,
free, works offline). AI-assisted mapping ("ask a model to assign palette roles to
site tokens") is explicitly **out of scope** for this task: the repo recently
removed its AI endpoints (`app/api/generate-preview`, `app/api/suggest-colors`),
and re-introducing a server AI dependency is a product decision. The mapping
module will expose a clean `SiteThemeVars` contract so an AI-backed generator can
be swapped in later without touching the apply mechanism.

### 3.3 Dark mode handling

The site's dark tokens come from a `@media` block, but inline `:root` overrides
win in both schemes. Therefore the mapping must be **scheme-aware**: compute a
light variant and a dark variant, pick based on `matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)")`,
and re-apply on scheme change while the toggle is ON (listener cleaned up on OFF).

### 3.4 Accessibility of mapped tokens

Foreground tokens (`--primary-foreground`, etc.) are chosen by WCAG contrast using
the existing helpers in `lib/color.ts` (pick white / near-black, whichever passes
against the mapped background stop). Never emit a foreground/background pair below
4.5:1 for text tokens.

## 4. Implementation steps

### Step A — `lib/site-theme.ts` (new, pure)

- `export type SiteThemeVars = Record<string, string>` (keys = the 21 flat tokens
  above + `--font-sans`, `--font-mono` when typography is applied).
- `export function siteThemeVars(input: { palettes: Palette[]; typography: Typography; scheme: "light" | "dark" }): SiteThemeVars`
- Mapping (light):
  - neutral scale (the role with preset `neutral`, else derive from primary):
    `--background` ← neutral-50, `--foreground` ← neutral-950,
    `--surface` ← white/neutral-50, `--surface-elevated` ← lightest stop,
    `--surface-muted` ← neutral-100, `--muted` ← neutral-500,
    `--border` ← neutral-200, `--input` ← neutral-300, `--overlay` ← neutral-950 @ alpha.
  - primary scale: `--primary` ← primary-600, `--primary-hover` ← primary-700,
    `--ring` ← primary-500, `--primary-foreground` ← contrast pick.
  - secondary/accent roles if present (else fall back to neutral/primary stops):
    `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--accent`, `--accent-foreground`,
    `--accent-subtle` (accent-100), `--accent-subtle-foreground` (accent-900).
  - error/destructive role if present, else leave `--destructive*` unset
    (site default remains).
  - Dark scheme: mirrored stop choices (background ← neutral-950, surface ←
    neutral-900, border ← neutral-800, primary ← primary-500, etc.).
  - Typography: `--font-sans` ← stack built from `typography.fonts.sans`
    (heading font intentionally not applied site-wide in MVP), `--font-mono` ←
    `typography.fonts.mono`.
- No DOM access in this module — pure and unit-testable.

### Step B — apply hook `components/generator/use-site-theme.ts` (new, client)

- `useSiteTheme(enabled: boolean, vars: { light: SiteThemeVars; dark: SiteThemeVars })`
- Effect: when `enabled`, resolve scheme via `matchMedia`, `setProperty` each var
  on `document.documentElement`, subscribe to scheme changes; keep the exact set of
  applied keys so cleanup `removeProperty`s only what we set.
- When vars change while enabled (user keeps editing colors), re-apply — live update.
- Cleanup on disable/unmount always restores defaults completely.

### Step C — toolbar toggle in `components/generator/palette-generator.tsx`

- New `const [applyToSite, setApplyToSite] = useState(false)`.
- Compute `siteVars = useMemo(() => ({ light: siteThemeVars({..., scheme:"light"}), dark: siteThemeVars({..., scheme:"dark"}) }), [palettes, typography])`.
- `useSiteTheme(applyToSite, siteVars)`.
- Toggle button in the right-hand `ml-auto` toolbar group (next to Preview,
  ~line 531): label "Apply to site", `aria-pressed`, visible active state,
  `data-testid="apply-to-site-toggle"`. Follow existing button styling.
- When ON and the applied sans font is a runtime Google Font, ensure it is loaded
  via the same mechanism as `font-preview-loader.tsx`.
- Session-only state (no persistence) for MVP.

### Step D — tests

- `__tests__/site-theme.test.ts` (new): token key coverage, light/dark stop
  selection, contrast-picked foregrounds, fallback behavior when optional roles
  are absent, font stack output.
- Extend `__tests__/palette-generator.test.tsx`: toggling ON sets expected
  properties on `document.documentElement`; editing a color while ON updates them;
  toggling OFF removes every property that was set; unmount cleans up.
- Optional e2e (`e2e/`): toggle and assert `body` computed background changes and
  reverts; follow existing `data-testid` conventions.

### Step E — quality gates

- `npm run test:unit`, lint, typecheck, production build must pass.
- Manual sanity: toggle on/off in light + dark OS scheme; no leaked vars.

## 5. Acceptance criteria

1. Toolbar shows an "Apply to site" toggle with correct pressed state and testid.
2. Toggling ON restyles the app chrome (background, surfaces, buttons, focus
   rings, fonts) from the current palette + typography, in both color schemes.
3. Edits to the palette while ON update the site theme live.
4. Toggling OFF (or unmounting the generator) restores the default theme exactly;
   no inline custom properties remain on `<html>`.
5. All mapped text token pairs meet WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1).
6. No server/API changes; no persistence; existing tests still pass; new unit +
   component tests cover the mapping and the apply/revert lifecycle.

## 6. Out of scope (explicit)

- AI-assisted token mapping (documented as future swap-in via `SiteThemeVars`).
- Iframe sandbox isolation.
- Persisting the toggle across reloads / into shared URLs.
- Per-page or partial application; heading-font site-wide application.
</file>

