## Environment

Public configuration lives in `lib/site-config.ts`. `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` is
the only env var consumed by site config; the Firebase and Google Analytics
variables are listed under [Accounts & Analytics](#accounts--analytics) below.

| Variable               | Required | Description                                            |
| ---------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` | No       | Absolute origin of the site. Defaults to `http://localhost:3000`. |

Copy it into a `.env.local` file for local overrides:

```bash
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://palettra.example
```

## Accounts & Analytics

Saved palettes use client-side Firebase Auth and Cloud Firestore. Create a
Firebase project, add a Web App, and copy the public web config values into
`.env.local`. Enable Google, Twitter, and Facebook sign-in providers in Firebase
Authentication. Twitter requires an API key and secret from the X developer
portal plus the callback URL shown by Firebase. Facebook requires a Meta app id
and secret. Add `localhost` and your production domain to Firebase authorized
domains.

Deploy the owner-only Firestore rules in this repo:

```bash
firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
```

Rules emulator tests with `@firebase/rules-unit-testing` are a recommended
follow-up, but they are not part of the default Vitest run because CI does not
start a Firestore emulator. The client still validates stored palette hrefs
before navigation.

For Google Analytics, create a GA4 property and Web data stream, then set the
measurement id. Next.js inlines `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values at build time, so changing
them only at runtime after `next build` has no effect.

| Variable                                    | Required | Description                                      |
| ------------------------------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY`              | Yes      | Firebase Web App API key.                        |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN`          | Yes      | Firebase Auth domain.                            |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`           | Yes      | Firebase project id for Auth and Firestore.      |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID`               | Yes      | Firebase Web App id.                             |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET`       | No       | Optional Firebase storage bucket.                |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID`  | No       | Optional Firebase messaging sender id.           |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID`             | No       | GA4 measurement id, e.g. `G-XXXXXXXXXX`.         |

Signed-in users can save, rename, open, and delete palettes. Saved palettes
include color roles and typography only; imported Stitch specs are not saved in
v1.

## Color roles

The generator supports multiple named color roles (Primary, Secondary, Tertiary,
Neutral, Error, or custom). Secondary/Tertiary/etc. can **auto-derive from the
Primary** via Material-style color harmony (hue rotation + per-role saturation,
in `lib/theme.ts`), or be set to a fixed color. The full theme is encoded into
the URL (`/generate/<primary>?colors=...`) so it is shareable.

The UI font is **Google Sans** (with Google Sans Code for monospace) via
`next/font/google`.

## SEO

All branding and SEO values are centralized in
[`lib/site-config.ts`](./lib/site-config.ts) — name, title, description,
keywords, author, theme colors, and social handle. Metadata, the manifest,
structured data, and the generated Open Graph / Twitter images all read from it,
so updating one file keeps everything in sync.

Non-production deployments are **non-indexable** by default: `robots.ts` and the
`robots` metadata only allow indexing when `NODE_ENV === "production"`, keeping
preview and staging environments out of search results.

## Testing

- **Unit** (`__tests__/`): Vitest + React Testing Library, jsdom environment.
- **End-to-end** (`e2e/`): Playwright across Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit. The
  Playwright config starts the Next.js server automatically.

```bash
npm run test:unit:run
npm run test:e2e
```

## Deployment

### Docker

The app builds to a standalone server (`output: "standalone"` in
`next.config.ts`).

```bash
docker build -t palettra .
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 palettra
```

### Vercel

Deploy directly to [Vercel](https://vercel.com/new). Set
`NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` to your production origin so canonical URLs, the sitemap,
and Open Graph tags resolve correctly.

## Commit Conventions

This project uses [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org).
Commit messages are validated by Commitlint via a Husky `commit-msg` hook, and
`git cz` / Commitizen offers an interactive prompt.
